  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1052 PM CST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nAn upper level storm system will continue to amplify over the \nsouthern high plains and progress across Oklahoma and Texas\nthrough Sunday. Light rain will develop Saturday afternoon and\nprogress eastward into Sunday morning. The heaviest rain is\nstill forecast along the Oklahoma line in closer proximity to\nthe upper level low. Temperatures will change only a few degrees\nthrough Monday morning given the extensive cloud, with mainly\nupper 30s to mid 40s day and night. So although daytime highs \nwill be chilly relative to climatology, overnight lows will be \nmild relative to climatology. The various ensemble suites still\nindicate the highest rain amounts near the Oklahoma state line \n(.25 to .5\") and lowest totals along I-70 where only a few \nhundredths are expected. And the grand ensemble continues to \nindicate very low chances of an inch or more of rain, even \nalong the Oklahoma line.\n\nAfter this system passes to the east, there will be no strong\nfront to scour out the cool and moist air, so expect only a\ngradual warmup Monday and Tuesday as winds become southerly, \nwith highs in the 40s Sunday under extensive cloud, and mid to\nupper 50s for Monday and Tuesday. An upper level trough is\nadvertised to pass to the north of Kansas Wednesday and shunt \nsome warmer air onto the high plains by Wednesday afternoon.\nTherefore, some locations in far western Kansas will likely \nexperience ample sun and readings in the 60s to near 70.\n\nAnother upper level trough is forecast to amplify over the\nwestern United States by late next week. Low level moisture\nwill stream back into western Kansas by Thursday and Friday as\nwinds remain southerly. There is considerable uncertainty in how\nfast this system will traverse the plains. A fast solution \nwould mean little to no rain for western Kansas, with low level \nmoisture being capped by dry mid level air. A slower solution \nwould mean deeper moisture and chances for showers and t-storms.\nThe grand ensemble shows very low chances ( less than 10%) for \n.5\" or more of rain during late week.",
